## Headline findings

1. **The full cache runs to 65,536 tokens on this GPU, and decode does not slow down with context.**
   Decode is {{ phase1.analysis.contexts.0.decode_tokens_per_s.median | .1f }} tokens/s at 4,096 tokens and
   {{ phase1.analysis.contexts.4.decode_tokens_per_s.median | .1f }} tokens/s at 65,536. Per-token time at
   65,536 is {{ phase1.analysis.decode_growth.last_over_first | .2f }}× that at 4,096. TTFT at 65,536
   tokens is {{ phase1.analysis.contexts.4.ttft_warm_s.median | .1f }} s.
2. **The prefetch window is {{ phase1.analysis.window_summary.window_over_phase0_bound_min | .0f }}–{{ phase1.analysis.window_summary.window_over_phase0_bound_max | .0f }}× wider than Phase 0's lower bound.**
   A decoder layer spans {{ phase1.analysis.window_summary.layer_window_min_s | ms }}–{{ phase1.analysis.window_summary.layer_window_max_s | ms }}
   on the GPU timeline at every context length. At the measured pinned H2D bandwidth, the KV of
   {{ phase1.analysis.window_summary.tokens_fetchable_min | int }}–{{ phase1.analysis.window_summary.tokens_fetchable_max | int }}
   tokens of one layer fits inside that span: **{{ phase1.analysis.window_summary.longest_ctx_fetchable_fraction | pct0 }} of a
   65,536-token context**, against {{ phase1.analysis.window_summary.longest_ctx_phase0_fraction | pct }} estimated in Phase 0.
   **Caveat:** at batch size 1 the span is mostly host-side kernel launching. Anything that
   removes host overhead (CUDA graphs, fused kernels) shrinks the window along with it.
3. **Decode is host-bound, so a residency policy cannot beat the full cache on latency here.**
   The attention kernel's share of a layer grows from {{ phase1.analysis.contexts.0.attention_kernel_share_of_layer | pct0 }}
   at 4,096 tokens to {{ phase1.analysis.contexts.4.attention_kernel_share_of_layer | pct0 }} at 65,536, yet
   per-token time stays flat (finding 1). The GPU finishes its work inside the time Python
   takes to launch it. On Llama-3.2-1B at batch size 1, shrinking the attended KV saves memory,
   not time. Every later latency comparison measures how much overhead a policy **adds**. This does
   not change the research question (the GPU budget was already an imposed constraint, per
   Phase 0), but it rules out a speed-up claim for this model. See the owner decisions below.
4. **Negative result: the reference was not repeatable on the fast kernel.** Running the
   same configuration twice gave top-1 agreement of
   {{ phase1.analysis.quality_first_run_summary.self_repeat_top1.min | pct }}–{{ phase1.analysis.quality_first_run_summary.self_repeat_top1.max | pct }}
   and mean KL of {{ phase1.analysis.quality_first_run_summary.self_repeat_kl.min | .1e }}–{{ phase1.analysis.quality_first_run_summary.self_repeat_kl.max | .1e }} nats.
   Cause: cuDNN's single-query SDPA returns different bits for identical inputs on
   {{ phase1.analysis.kernel_repeatability.by_strategy.cudnn_bucketed.not_repeatable }} of
   {{ phase1.analysis.kernel_repeatability.by_strategy.cudnn_bucketed.calls | int }} real decode calls.
   The memory-efficient kernel is bit-repeatable, so quality is now scored on it, and the
   reference is bit-identical to itself at every measured context (§2).
5. **Negative result: Windows moved the benchmark onto E-cores.** Under the OS's default power
   throttling, the detached benchmark process ran slower tokens almost entirely on E-cores:
   {{ phase1.analysis.latency_regimes.throttling_median_ratio | .2f }}× the median and
   {{ phase1.analysis.latency_regimes.throttling_p90_ratio | .2f }}× the p90 of the opted-out
   condition. nvidia-smi polling had no effect ({{ phase1.analysis.latency_regimes.nvidia_smi_median_ratio | .2f }}×).
   The first baseline mixed both regimes. All timing benchmarks now opt out, and the baseline
   was rerun.

